   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Basal Metabolic Rate causes Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct relationship. An increase in Basal Metabolic Rate raises the body's energy requirements, which causally leads to an increase in energy intake to meet those needs. Conversely, a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate reduces daily energy requirements, resulting in lower energy intake. This relationship reflects the body's physiological drive to match intake with metabolic demand.", 'session_id': '211cfbbd-4f59-4b89-94a1-04cc38009cfc', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "A higher Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) means the body uses more calories at rest, which could signal the body to compensate by reducing overall food intake in order to maintain energy balance. Therefore, as BMR increases, the Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) should decrease, reflecting the body's natural tendency to avoid excessive caloric intake when metabolic demands are higher.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': "A higher Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) means the body uses more calories at rest, which could signal the body to compensate by reducing overall food intake in order to maintain energy balance. Therefore, as BMR increases, the Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) should decrease, reflecting the body's natural tendency to avoid excessive caloric intake when metabolic demands are higher.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

