64. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI-[POSITIVE]->Norm BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes Norm BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. As the socio-cultural ideal for BMI increases, the socially accepted norm BMI tends to rise because people adjust perceptions of what is considered normal based on cultural ideals. Conversely, a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI leads to a decrease in norm BMI. This relationship is direct and does not require mediating variables from the list provided.', 'session_id': '211cfbbd-4f59-4b89-94a1-04cc38009cfc', 'prob_std': 0.3088771314593597, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7162509034851211, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI influences Norm BMI through a physiological adaptation process, whereby extended exposure to a prevailing ideal BMI in media and public discourse induces gradual biological changes within the population. Over time, individuals’ metabolic rates and appetite regulation systems are subconsciously calibrated to align with the predominant Socio-cultural Ideal BMI, resulting in a corresponding shift in Norm BMI. This mechanism operates independently of individual behavior or conscious social comparison, suggesting that changes in Socio-cultural Ideal BMI are directly internalized at the physiological level, thereby causing parallel adjustments in Norm BMI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI influences Norm BMI through a physiological adaptation process, whereby extended exposure to a prevailing ideal BMI in media and public discourse induces gradual biological changes within the population. Over time, individuals’ metabolic rates and appetite regulation systems are subconsciously calibrated to align with the predominant Socio-cultural Ideal BMI, resulting in a corresponding shift in Norm BMI. This mechanism operates independently of individual behavior or conscious social comparison, suggesting that changes in Socio-cultural Ideal BMI are directly internalized at the physiological level, thereby causing parallel adjustments in Norm BMI.', 'perplexity': 1.5776048184286398, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7569542416309964, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01569997550914131, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003748631823178404, 'prob_variance': 0.09540508233856258, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.066048970509217, 'avg_nll': 0.45590775913935855}

