105. Group-level BMI-[POSITIVE]->Norm BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through the process of social comparison and norm-setting. As the average BMI within a group increases, the perceived 'normal' BMI (Norm BMI) also tends to increase, since individuals adjust their expectations based on the group average. Conversely, a decrease in group-level BMI lowers the norm BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on how social norms are established.", 'session_id': '211cfbbd-4f59-4b89-94a1-04cc38009cfc', 'prob_std': 0.2914302869169425, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5358078/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6798544748920335, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI because higher Group-level BMI leads to increased environmental exposure to higher body weights, which in turn affects metabolic adaptation at the population level. Through repeated visual and physiological exposure to elevated body mass within a group, individuals' baseline metabolic set points shift upward, causing the collective perception of a healthy or 'normal' BMI—reflected in the Norm BMI—to rise as well. Thus, changes in Group-level BMI directly recalibrate physiological norms through adaptation, leading to corresponding shifts in Norm BMI.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI because higher Group-level BMI leads to increased environmental exposure to higher body weights, which in turn affects metabolic adaptation at the population level. Through repeated visual and physiological exposure to elevated body mass within a group, individuals' baseline metabolic set points shift upward, causing the collective perception of a healthy or 'normal' BMI—reflected in the Norm BMI—to rise as 